{
  "summary": "The previous proposal's recommended fix (option a: setting Environment=MAD_DIR in the systemd unit) is incorrect because MAD_DIR is only used by AgentRunner as a fallback for _workdir (the directory where agents execute), NOT by Config() for locating config or lock files. Config.__init__() calls find_config() which walks from cwd — it never reads MAD_DIR. So setting MAD_DIR would not change where lock files are created. The actual root cause has two parts: (1) find_config() falls back to ~/MAD/config.json when no local .mad/config.json exists in the project tree, causing Config.mad_dir to resolve to ~/MAD for ALL projects without local configs, and (2) the service install flow (service.py install_service) never calls ensure_local_config() for the target project directory, so ~/chickentracks.app never gets its own .mad/config.json. A correct fix would either: (a) have install_service() call ensure_local_config(project_dir) so each project gets its own .mad/config.json and thus its own lock file namespace, or (b) make the lock file path incorporate the project directory hash (e.g., .pipeline-lock.{project_hash}.tui) so locks are inherently project-scoped regardless of config resolution, or (c) pass an explicit config path to Config() in the service entry point rather than relying on cwd-based discovery. Option (a) is simplest but has a subtle gap: if WorkingDirectory is set correctly in the unit file but the service binary (pipeline tui) re-resolves config via find_config() before cwd is fully established, the race could still hit the global fallback. Option (b) is the most robust since it decouples lock isolation from config resolution entirely."
}
